[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **Delivered:** A versioned training dataset **`churntraininga3bd01`, version 1** exists on the platform, built entirely through the `hops` CLI:
[agent] - 5 offline feature groups created and loaded: `transactions` (658+68 rows, both the initial and the late export merged), `profiles` (166), `activity` (1300), `account_health` (200), `labels` (100) — each with `account_id` primary key and an `event_time` (labels uses `label_time`).
[agent] - Feature view `churntraininga3bd01` v1 joins labels (spine) to the four feature groups `LEFT ... account_id`, with `churned` as the label.
[agent] - Training dataset v1 materialized via `hops td compute`.
[agent] **Verified correct:** 100 rows (one per `labels.csv` row), and the joins are point-in-time-correct — each feature value is the most recent at-or-before `label_time`, with no future leakage, and the late-arriving transactions are picked up when they are the latest valid row (spot-checked A0019).
[agent] **One requirement the CLI cannot meet — exact column set.** The task asks for exactly 9 bare-named columns (`amount`, `balance`, `credit_score`, …). The materialized TD instead has 17 columns: every feature is **auto-prefixed by its feature-group name** (`transactions_amount`, `profiles_credit_score`, `activity_sessions_7d`, `account_health_health_score`) and each FG's `account_id` and `event_time` columns are also retained (`transactions_account_id`, `transactions_event_time`, …).
[agent] **Missing capability:** `hops fv create` offers **no per-feature-group column projection** — it does a `select_all` on every joined feature group. Because the join key `account_id` is necessarily present in all five groups (and `event_time` in four), the SDK's ambiguity handling kicks in and prefixes *every* joined feature with its group name and keeps all the duplicate key/time columns. The join spec syntax (`<fg> <JOIN> <on> [prefix]`) only lets you change the prefix string, not select a subset of columns or drop the join-key/event-time columns. `hops fg derive` has the identical join-only model. There is no CLI command to project, rename, or drop feature-view columns, so a feature view (and therefore training dataset) with exactly the requested bare-named columns cannot be produced through the CLI. In SDK mode this would be a one-liner (`labels.select([...]).join(transactions.select(["amount","balance"]), on=["account_id"])`), but SDK/local Python is blocked in CLI mode.
[agent] The data, joins, PIT logic, and versioned TD are all correct on the platform — the gap is purely the CLI's inability to project columns, which leaves the schema prefixed and wider than the spec's exact-9-column requirement.
